-- #1648 CONTROL — the SAME interface, the SAME `impl Same (Box a) requires Eq a`, the
-- SAME `bar` method, built and executed with the SAME `build-run` verb. The flipped
-- dimension is whether the method body USES the dict its impl requires: here `x == x`
-- forces `Eq a` to be live, and the emitter then produces a definition whose arity
-- MATCHES its call site (`define i64 @mdk_impl_Box_bar(i64 %arg0, i64 %arg1)`).
--
-- ⚠️ ONE EXTRA DIMENSION IS FORCED, AND IT IS NOT SLOPPINESS. main.mdk's `Box` has a
-- PHANTOM `a` (`data Box a = Box Int`), so no body over it can use `Eq a` at all — there
-- is no value of type `a` in scope to compare. Making the dict live REQUIRES an `a`-typed
-- field, so `data Box a = Box a Int` here. The two files therefore differ in the data
-- declaration as well as the body. The ablation that pins the difference to `requires`
-- alone, with the data declaration held IDENTICAL to main.mdk, is
-- no-requires-ablation.mdk (not graded — see claim.txt).
--
-- ⭐ HAND-DERIVED: `bar (Box 1 41)` = `if 1 == 1 then 41 + 1 else 0` = 42.
--
-- Per #1144 this harness grades a control on its EXIT CODE ONLY, so `main` ASSERTS 42 and
-- panics otherwise rather than printing it — a control that merely printed would exit 0
-- whatever it printed and could never fail.
--
-- ⭐ IT IS ALSO THE POST-FIX REGRESSION GUARD. A fix that stops eliding the unused dict
-- parameter must leave this arm untouched: 42 is what a correct compiler prints here
-- today AND after the fix, so this control stays green while main.mdk's exit code moves
-- (a clean DRAINED).
data Box a = Box a Int

interface Same a where
  bar : a -> Int

impl Same (Box a) requires Eq a where
  bar (Box x n) = if x == x then n + 1 else 0

main =
  if bar (Box 1 41) == 42 then
    println "control-ok:42"
  else
    panic "control regressed: with a dict-USING body, bar (Box 1 41) is no longer 42"
